1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:04,410
And the next one is present, continuous, present, continuous.

2
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:10,590
Remember, the formation is the verb be plus verb with -ing ending.

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:19,140
So be means is, am, are or am, is, are. The usage.

4
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:23,880
It's used to talk about future arrangements.

5
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:26,250
And what is an arrangement?

6
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:31,650
Arrangement is a plan that you have decided on and organized.

7
00:00:32,310 --> 00:00:37,590
The point here is what is the difference between a plan and the arrangement?

8
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:38,760
The plan is I

9
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:40,740
I said to myself

10
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:44,730
I'm going to go to the theater tomorrow.

11
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:53,440
And an arrangement is when I have the tickets already bought and I have the date and the time where

12
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:54,790
the play starts.

13
00:00:55,330 --> 00:01:02,800
Then in this case, it's already an arrangement because I have arranged it, I have organised everything

14
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,360
or things like that.

15
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:07,810
So this is the difference between plan and arrangement.

16
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:14,950
Now let's look at examples. Raluca's leaving on Friday and coming back on Tuesday.

17
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:18,390
We know she has those tickets.

18
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,000
We know she has organised everything, arranged everything.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,490
So it's pretty much definite.

20
00:01:25,030 --> 00:01:28,600
And because it's very definite.

21
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:34,390
That's why we actually, we speak about the future.

22
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:44,380
But we we're actually using present tense because it's as if it's already done, as if it's already

23
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:45,430
happening.

24
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:52,570
That's why we use we we mean future, but we say present.

25
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:55,540
And this is a very common way.

26
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:56,680
How to speak about

27
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:04,360
those arrangements or those very close future things that will happen.

28
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:10,780
Yeah, we know they will happen, because they're all arranged and organized and that's why we don't even

29
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,940
want to speak about them in future tense.

30
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:19,630
But we just take the present tense and, and say it as, as if it's already happening.

31
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:23,710
Okay, so pay attention to this grammar structure here.

32
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:31,510
Raluca is leaving on Friday and coming back on Tuesday, is leaving and is coming.

33
00:02:33,910 --> 00:02:34,990
Okay. The next one.

34
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:37,210
What are you doing later today?

35
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:40,630
I'm seeing my granny. Again.

36
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:42,790
This is an arrangement.

37
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:47,830
You have already discussed this with your granny.

38
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:51,760
Okay. So she knows you're going to come.

39
00:02:51,790 --> 00:02:53,620
She is expecting you.

40
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:56,050
That's why it's very definite.

41
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,880
You are very sure that this is how you are spending your evening today.

42
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:04,270
That's why you say I am seeing my granny.

43
00:03:04,270 --> 00:03:06,460
It's not your plan.

44
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:09,220
It's already something definite.

45
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:11,050
It's a very definite plan.

46
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:23,350
Now, the thing that you can also use here with present continuous is that you can also use these phrases

47
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:35,320
hoping to, planning to, plus infinitive or thinking of plus verb with -ing, hoping to infinitive,

48
00:03:35,650 --> 00:03:40,180
planning to infinitive or thinking of something.

49
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,280
And we use a verb with -ing.

50
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:54,640
For example, Maria is hoping to get certified in DevOps this year, is hoping to get certified.

51
00:03:56,230 --> 00:04:02,380
So again, this is her plan, let's say.

52
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:13,390
Yeah, it's not definite arrangement because here we we see that the word hope comes to play or plan.

53
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:22,390
So again, this is not a definite arrangement, but this is a way how to use how to speak about those

54
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:30,280
less certain things in the future, using those words that give us this feeling of less certainty,

55
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:34,090
hoping to, planning to or thinking of.

56
00:04:34,930 --> 00:04:37,660
Are you thinking of applying for that position?

57
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:47,080
Again, the person asks if I have a plan or if I am considering applying for that position.

58
00:04:48,010 --> 00:04:48,760
All right.

59
00:04:49,270 --> 00:04:52,540
The next point here and now

60
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:53,740
here it comes.

61
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:55,180
Future simple.

62
00:04:56,290 --> 00:05:00,490
Yes, we use Future Simple to talk about future.

63
00:05:01,630 --> 00:05:04,840
The formation is will plus infinitive.

64
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:14,620
You know that. Usage. Used for instant decisions, promises, offers, predictions and future facts.

65
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:23,050
So now try to try to understand this point here.

66
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,120
Really think about it.

67
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:30,760
We don't use future simple for everything that we speak about

68
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:37,120
that is about future, not about every future action or future plan or whatever.

69
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:44,140
We only use future simple for those things. For instant decisions,

70
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:48,640
promises, for offers, for predictions and for future facts.

71
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:50,950
All right.

72
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:52,570
Let's look at the examples.

73
00:05:53,110 --> 00:05:54,580
Have you seen my report?

74
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:57,250
I'll have a look at it later today.

75
00:05:57,280 --> 00:05:59,590
What is it? It's an instant decision.

76
00:06:01,030 --> 00:06:08,200
I decided right away just now, right here and right now, I decided I will have a look at it later

77
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:08,800
today.

78
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:10,390
It's not my plan.

79
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:12,640
I haven't planned that.

80
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:17,410
It's what I decided right now about the future.

81
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,330
So instant decision.

82
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:23,020
I'll call you after the meeting to give you an update.

83
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:26,080
Promise. The next one.

84
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:26,740
Sure.

85
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:29,770
I'll stand in for you while you are on training session.

86
00:06:29,770 --> 00:06:30,850
It's not a problem.

87
00:06:31,270 --> 00:06:31,930
What is it?

88
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:33,010
It's an offer.

89
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:35,170
I offer something

90
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:40,190
about the future, in the future that I will do something in the future.

91
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:43,190
I heard you got promoted.

92
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:43,900
Congrats.

93
00:06:43,910 --> 00:06:45,530
You'll be a good team leader.

94
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:47,240
It's a prediction.

95
00:06:48,050 --> 00:06:53,750
Now, at this point, let's compare two types of predictions.

96
00:06:54,140 --> 00:07:03,380
Remember when we spoke about "be going to plus infinitive", we said that it's also used for predictions.

97
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:13,130
But I asked you to specifically focus on the point here that these predictions have to be logically

98
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:16,400
based on something, like here in this sentence.

99
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:18,920
Look at those clouds. It's going to rain.

100
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:23,780
So this prediction is based on some evidence.

101
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:33,890
And the prediction here, the one that we are speaking about in this sentence, is not based on any

102
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:34,790
evidence.

103
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:44,930
It's just what I think, it's just my thought, my opinion about something or somebody. When it's not based

104
00:07:44,930 --> 00:07:48,680
on any evidence, like visible evidence.

105
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,420
And it's just my thought.

106
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:58,280
This is a prediction, but we use future simple for those kinds of predictions.

107
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,460
I think that's clear.

108
00:08:01,550 --> 00:08:08,750
And the last example here. Next year, your company will hire a lot of new employees.

109
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:10,640
It's a future fact.

110
00:08:11,810 --> 00:08:17,540
Future facts are usually things like big, big things.

111
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:23,720
It's not about some smaller plans or intentions.

112
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:29,270
It's not about my next year vacation.

113
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:33,559
It's not about what I'm going to do this evening.

114
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:42,289
It's more about bigger things where we speak about, I don't know, politics or economics, or when

115
00:08:42,289 --> 00:08:53,000
we discuss what inventions the people are going to make in the future, how the future life will look

116
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:53,540
like.

117
00:08:54,020 --> 00:09:03,770
So these kinds of future facts that may be based on some, I don't know, research statistics or things

118
00:09:03,770 --> 00:09:04,490
like that.

119
00:09:06,410 --> 00:09:11,930
The next point is future simple, but the other kind.

120
00:09:13,310 --> 00:09:18,140
Like a sub-category of future simple.

121
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:21,650
It's the sentences that use the word shall.

122
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:23,090
What is it?

123
00:09:23,090 --> 00:09:23,840
Shall.

124
00:09:24,110 --> 00:09:25,910
Shall plus infinitive.

125
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:30,200
We use it for offers and suggestions.

126
00:09:30,860 --> 00:09:33,500
Offers and suggestions for the future

127
00:09:33,710 --> 00:09:36,200
is "shall plus infinitive".

128
00:09:36,410 --> 00:09:43,520
But the point is that we only use shall in interrogative sentences, so only in questions.

129
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:47,090
We cannot use it in positive sentences or negative sentences.

130
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:53,330
We use them with questions and they sound something like that.

131
00:09:53,390 --> 00:09:55,820
Shall I take the meeting minutes today?

132
00:09:56,510 --> 00:09:57,590
It's an offer.

133
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:02,000
Or Shall we share the tasks so we can finish earlier?

134
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:04,100
It's a suggestion.

135
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,670
So shall is only in questions, only for office, for suggestions.

136
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:14,600
Now, what is the difference between an offer and a suggestion?

137
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:20,630
When I offer, this is something that I will do.

138
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:24,050
This is something that I will do for somebody.

139
00:10:24,050 --> 00:10:24,950
For example.

140
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:27,150
Shall I take the meeting minutes?

141
00:10:27,170 --> 00:10:32,000
I offer my my services for that.

142
00:10:32,390 --> 00:10:38,510
And the suggestion is I offer for us to do something together.

143
00:10:38,510 --> 00:10:41,150
For example, shall we share the tasks?

144
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:45,620
Not me personally, but shall we do something together

145
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:47,380
So shall we...?

146
00:10:47,390 --> 00:10:49,070
It's always a suggestion.

147
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:51,100
Good.

148
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:54,980
And the last point here is present

149
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:55,850
simple.

150
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:57,770
Because simple is interesting,

151
00:10:57,770 --> 00:11:02,780
because present simple is actually very present tense.

152
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:07,640
But there are cases when we use present simple.

153
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:17,180
And those cases are maybe a bit rare, but they are used in very specific situations.

154
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:24,020
And these specific situations are schedules, timetables, itineraries and things like that.

155
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:26,140
What does it mean?

156
00:11:26,150 --> 00:11:37,820
We have a schedule and, for example, a schedule of or a timetable of buses that go through a station.

157
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:51,110
And when we read this timetable, we usually don't say that the bus will come at 8 am or something

158
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:51,740
like that.

159
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:56,870
We say that "it comes" because this is regularity.

160
00:11:58,010 --> 00:11:59,600
It's a regular action.

161
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:05,930
It's something that is given and it's certain and it's, it's like that.

162
00:12:05,930 --> 00:12:07,430
It doesn't change.

163
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:07,540
Yeah.

164
00:12:07,610 --> 00:12:10,670
Like generally it doesn't really change.

165
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:14,780
So we expect that this is certain.

166
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:22,910
So that's why for tables, timetables, schedules and itineraries, we just use present simple.

167
00:12:23,210 --> 00:12:27,920
For example, daily stand-up starts at 8 AM and finishes at 8:15.

168
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:30,200
This is something regular.

169
00:12:30,410 --> 00:12:32,120
It happens every day.

170
00:12:32,390 --> 00:12:35,870
So we speak about it in simple present tense.

171
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:39,530
The semester begins in October.

172
00:12:39,770 --> 00:12:41,510
Again, this is given.

173
00:12:42,380 --> 00:12:44,600
The train leaves at 8 AM.

174
00:12:44,810 --> 00:12:45,640
This is given.

175
00:12:45,650 --> 00:12:46,790
This is just like that.

176
00:12:46,790 --> 00:12:48,050
It's a timetable.

177
00:12:48,050 --> 00:12:49,070
It's a schedule.

178
00:12:50,690 --> 00:12:56,390
So this is where you can use present simple for future things.

179
00:12:57,560 --> 00:12:58,820
And this is.

180
00:12:58,820 --> 00:12:59,600
This is it.

181
00:12:59,630 --> 00:13:00,530
This is it.

182
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:03,980
So we have six.

183
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:05,840
Six

184
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:11,000
options what to use for future.

185
00:13:11,390 --> 00:13:19,100
The first one was to be going to, used for plans, intentions and predictions based on evidence.

186
00:13:19,370 --> 00:13:27,830
We have an option to use might, could or may when we are less sure of our plans.

187
00:13:28,490 --> 00:13:38,120
We use present continuous when we speak about arrangements, when it's all definite, we have the tickets,

188
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:40,190
it's all arranged and organized.

189
00:13:40,190 --> 00:13:42,350
We can use present continuous.

190
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:47,720
We use future simple for these categories:

191
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:53,600
instant decisions, promises, offers, suggestions, predictions, future facts.

192
00:13:54,380 --> 00:14:01,160
We use future simple with shall specifically for offers and suggestions and always in questions.

193
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:06,350
And we use present sample for schedules, timetables, itineraries.

194
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:08,480
Good.

195
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:14,840
That was a lot, but I hope that was good information for you.

196
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:21,260
The last thing that we would like to do is an exercise.

197
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:24,860
So I will give you a couple of minutes here.

198
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:35,030
Try to look at those sentences and try to complete all of them, complete the gaps.

199
00:14:35,390 --> 00:14:39,740
I will give you an example for the first sentence, so it's more clear.

200
00:14:40,670 --> 00:14:46,970
We have a couple of gaps here and we have you study all day tomorrow.

201
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:48,260
And it's a question.

202
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:57,650
So we know it's a plan or intention and we know that for plans and intentions we use, what do we use?

203
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:02,180
Yes, we use be going to plus infinitive.

204
00:15:02,540 --> 00:15:04,010
So we say.

205
00:15:06,930 --> 00:15:08,100
Are you

206
00:15:10,770 --> 00:15:16,290
going to study all day tomorrow?

207
00:15:16,290 --> 00:15:17,520
And this is our

208
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,940
correct sentence.

209
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:21,420
Now.

210
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:22,860
I will give you a couple of minutes.

211
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:28,740
Please look at the rest of them and then I will give you the answers.

212
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:46,170
Okay.

213
00:15:46,170 --> 00:15:51,090
So let's look at the rest of the sentences.

214
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:59,040
If you need more time, you can pause the video here and finish the task.

215
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:03,690
And I will try to deal with the rest of the sentences.

216
00:16:03,870 --> 00:16:06,420
So the next one is It

217
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:11,130
and we have the verb rain the whole day, the whole next week.

218
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:16,830
I saw the forecast. And we know it's a future fact.

219
00:16:19,410 --> 00:16:21,270
Speaking about future facts

220
00:16:21,270 --> 00:16:24,360
we know that we use future simple.

221
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:29,430
So we would say it will rain the whole next week.

222
00:16:30,060 --> 00:16:31,250
I saw the forecast.

223
00:16:31,260 --> 00:16:33,060
It means it's a future fact.

224
00:16:35,190 --> 00:16:38,730
We could also say that it's a prediction.

225
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:44,070
It depends on how we look at the situation.

226
00:16:44,370 --> 00:16:47,400
So we can say that it's a prediction.

227
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:55,080
I saw the forecast or I saw the the clouds coming or something like that.

228
00:16:55,080 --> 00:16:55,260
Yes.

229
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:58,140
So it's again, it could be a prediction as well.

230
00:16:58,140 --> 00:17:04,770
And if we want to make this a prediction, we would use the same structure as in the first sentence,

231
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:11,910
sentence, and we would say it is going to rain the whole next week.

232
00:17:13,950 --> 00:17:17,010
The next one we have the verb leave.

233
00:17:17,730 --> 00:17:22,970
So on Thursday and come back on Friday afternoon.

234
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:23,970
What is it?

235
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,130
It's a future arrangement.

236
00:17:26,220 --> 00:17:33,540
We know that for arrangements we usually use present continuous, because it's very, very definite.

237
00:17:34,830 --> 00:17:36,420
Present continuous.

238
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:37,650
Let's see.

239
00:17:37,650 --> 00:17:39,090
What is the formation?

240
00:17:39,270 --> 00:17:42,840
I am leaving

241
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:48,240
on Thursday and

242
00:17:50,910 --> 00:17:54,450
coming back Friday afternoon.

243
00:17:54,900 --> 00:18:03,090
Remember that when you have this situation, when you have two verbs, you only use the auxiliary verb

244
00:18:03,090 --> 00:18:03,600
once.

245
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:07,800
So you say, I am leaving and coming.

246
00:18:07,890 --> 00:18:12,390
You could also say, and I am coming back.

247
00:18:12,390 --> 00:18:18,920
So you can just repeat this part twice, but it's not really used.

248
00:18:18,930 --> 00:18:23,010
You don't need to use it twice so you can do it just like that.

249
00:18:23,010 --> 00:18:27,210
I am leaving, blah blah and coming and so on.

250
00:18:28,350 --> 00:18:32,550
The next one. We are talking about an instant decision here.

251
00:18:34,230 --> 00:18:38,240
Have a cappuccino and a cheesecake, please.

252
00:18:38,250 --> 00:18:44,850
So the situation is I'm at a cafe and I order something.

253
00:18:45,510 --> 00:18:49,740
So this is a typical instant decision.

254
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:56,970
I came to the cafe, I looked at the menu and I decided: Hmm, I will have a cappuccino and a cheesecake

255
00:18:56,970 --> 00:18:57,530
please.

256
00:18:58,410 --> 00:18:59,670
Instant decisions.

257
00:18:59,670 --> 00:19:01,110
We know that we use

258
00:19:01,110 --> 00:19:02,760
will for those.

259
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:14,040
The next one. We have to go to a stand-up comedy show later tonight.

260
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:18,390
And here in brackets, I gave you a hint.

261
00:19:18,540 --> 00:19:24,480
The hint is I'm not sure yet, so I'm considering this.

262
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:26,640
Maybe I will go.

263
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:28,260
Maybe I will not go.

264
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:29,310
I'm not sure.

265
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:37,770
And remember, for things that are less certain, less definite, we can use either might or may or

266
00:19:37,770 --> 00:19:38,400
could.

267
00:19:39,610 --> 00:19:44,710
So let's take a nice word might and we have our sentence.

268
00:19:44,710 --> 00:19:50,170
I might go to a standup comedy show later tonight, but I'm not sure yet.

269
00:19:51,490 --> 00:19:51,780
Good.

270
00:19:51,790 --> 00:19:56,610
And the next one, the show, the verb start.

271
00:19:56,620 --> 00:20:00,580
And we have 8 AM, what is it?

272
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:07,090
It's a timetable, a timetable or a schedule?

273
00:20:08,770 --> 00:20:17,140
And for times, tables and schedules, you know that we use present simple because it's something recurring,

274
00:20:17,140 --> 00:20:18,190
repeated.

275
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:25,480
So we know it's about the future, but we just use present simple tense.

276
00:20:25,510 --> 00:20:27,610
The show starts at 8 PM.

277
00:20:29,090 --> 00:20:32,390
Okay, to make it more visible,

278
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:38,180
we can say the show always starts at 8 PM.

279
00:20:38,750 --> 00:20:43,130
To make it more clear that it's actually a timetable.

280
00:20:46,190 --> 00:20:46,610
Good.

281
00:20:47,510 --> 00:20:48,890
So well done here.

282
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:51,290
Thank you for your attention.

283
00:20:52,010 --> 00:20:54,680
Please do the tasks after the lessons.

284
00:20:54,680 --> 00:20:56,390
And we will go on.

